A pipe input's stop empties the pipe before closing it.

The engine keeps a second read descriptor on every pipe it watches for
autostart, so closing the input's descriptor never drops what the pipe
still holds: up to 64 KB, a third of a second of audio the writer sent
before it stopped. On a pause the input is stopped and set up again at
once, and that audio was the first thing read, and the first thing heard
on resume, from ahead of where the sound had stopped, followed by the
resume proper from the right place. It sounded like a crackle. The writer
has always stopped before we get here (librespot closes its end before it
reports paused), and what it writes next begins where its own metadata
says, so what is queued at a stop is never wanted.

